"hadiths[29]{hadithnumber,text}:"
1146,Aishah narrated that the Messenger of Allah said:“Indeed Allah has made unlawful through suckling whatever He made unlawful through birth.”
1147,Aishah narrated:“My uncle through suckling came and asked permission (to enter) but I refused to admit him until I asked the Messenger of Allah. So the Messenger of Allah said: “Let him in since he is your uncle.’” She said: “It is only the woman who suckled me; I was not suckled by the man.’ So he said: ‘Indeed he is your uncle
1148,"Mahmud ibn Ghaylan narrated to us: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from ‘Alqama, from Ibn Mas‘ud, that he was asked about a man who married a woman but did not specify a dowry (sadaq) for her and did not consummate the marriage before he died. Ibn Mas‘ud said: “She is entitled to a dowry equal to that of her peers—neither reduced nor excessive—and she must observe the waiting period (‘iddah), and she inherits from him.” Then Ma‘qil ibn Sinan al-Ashja‘i stood up and said: “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ruled regarding Birwa‘ bint Waashiq, a woman from among us, exactly as you have ruled.” Ibn Mas‘ud was delighted by this. (Al-Tirmidhi said:) There are narrations on this topic from al-Jarrah. 
Al-Hasan ibn ‘Ali al-Khallal narrated to us: Yazid ibn Harun and ‘Abd al-Razzaq both narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Mansur, a similar narration. Abu ‘Isa said: The hadith of Ibn Mas‘ud is hasan sahih, and it has been narrated from him through multiple chains. This is the practice followed by some of the scholars among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ and others. This is the view of al-Thawri, Ahmad, and Ishaq. 
However, some scholars among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ—including ‘Ali ibn Abi Talib, Zayd ibn Thabit, Ibn ‘Abbas, and Ibn ‘Umar—held that if a man marries a woman, does not consummate the marriage, and dies without specifying a dowry, then she inherits, but she is not entitled to a dowry, and she must observe the waiting period. This was the view of al-Shafi‘i, who said: “If the hadith of Birwa‘ bint Waashiq were firmly established, the proof would lie in what was narrated from the Prophet ﷺ.” It is also reported that al-Shafi‘i later retracted this opinion in Egypt and affirmed the ruling based on the hadith of Birwa‘ bint Waashiq."
1149,Amr bin Ash-Shariq narrated that Ibn Abbas was asked about the case in which a man had two slave girls
1150,Abdullah bin Az-Zubair narrated from Aishah from the Prophet who said:“One sip or two sips do not make a prohibition.”
"1151","“My uncle through suckling came and asked permission (to enter) but I refused to admit him until I asked the Messenger of Allah. So the Messenger of Allah said: “Let him in since he is your uncle.’” She said: “It is only the woman who suckled me; I was not suckled by the man.’ So he said: ‘Indeed he is your uncle, so let him in.’”"
1152,Umm Salamah narrated that The Messenger of Allah said:“No prohibition results from suckling except for what penetrates the intestines while on the breast before weaning.”
1153,Hajjaj bin Hajjaj Al-Aslami narrated that His father asked the Prophet:“O Messenger of Allah! What will remove the responsibility of the foster relationship from me?” So he said: “A Ghurrah: a male slave or a female slave.”
1154,"# Translation of the Hadith

**Narrated by 'Uqbah ibn al-Harith (may Allah be pleased with him):**

He said: ""I married a woman, and then a black woman came to us and said, 'Indeed, I have suckled both of you (i.e., you and your wife, thereby making you foster-relatives).' So I went to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and said, 'I married the daughter of so-and-so, and then a black woman came to us and said, ""Indeed, I have suckled both of you,"" and she is lying.'""

He said: ""The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) turned away from me."" He said: ""So I came to him from the direction of his face, and he turned his face away from me. So I said, 'Indeed, she is lying.'""

He (the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him) said: **'How can you (keep) her, when she has claimed that she suckled both of you? Leave her (i.e., separate from your wife).'**

---

**Abu 'Isa (al-Tirmidhi) said:** The hadith of 'Uqbah ibn al-Harith is a *hasan sahith* (good and authentic) hadith. And there is a narration on this topic from Ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both).

**Abu 'Isa said:** More than one narrator reported this hadith from Ibn Abi Mulaykah from 'Uqbah ibn al-Harith, and they did not mention in it the chain through 'Ubayd ibn Abi Maryam, and they did not include the phrase **""Leave her.""**

The practice (established ruling) according to this hadith is held by some of the people of knowledge among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and others: they permitted the testimony of a single woman regarding suckling (radha'ah). Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) said: ""The testimony of a single woman is accepted regarding suckling, and her oath is taken."" This is the position of Ahmad and Ishaq.

However, some of the people of knowledge said: ""The testimony of a single woman is not accepted until there are more (witnesses)."" This is the position of al-Shafi'i.

I heard al-Jarud say: I heard Waki' say: ""The testimony of a single woman is not accepted in legal judgment, but one separates from her (the wife) out of scrupulous caution (wara')."""
1155,"# Hadith on the Conditions of Breastfeeding (Ridāʿah) Establishing Mahram Status

**Narration Chain:**

Qutaybah narrated to us — Abū ʿAwānah narrated to us — from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah — from Fāṭimah bint al-Mundhir — from Umm Salamah (may Allāh be pleased with her), who reported:

**The Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam) said:**

*""That which establishes the prohibition (taḥrīm) of breastfeeding is only that which intestines open (i.e., milk that nourishes the stomach) from the breast, and this occurs before weaning.""*

---

**Commentary of Imām Abū ʿĪsā (al-Tirmidhī, raḥimahullāh):**

Abū ʿĪsā (may Allāh have mercy upon him) said: **""This is a ḥasan ṣaḥīḥ hadith.""**

He further stated: **""The established practice (al-ʿamal) according to the majority of the scholars from among the Companions of the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam) and others is that breastfeeding does not establish the prohibition of marriage (taḥrīm) except what occurs within the first two years of age. Whatever breastfeeding takes place after the completion of the full two years, it does not establish any prohibition whatsoever.""**

He also noted: **""Fāṭimah bint al-Mundhir ibn al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām was the wife of Hishām ibn ʿUrwah.""**

---

### Scholarly Notes:

This hadith addresses the jurisprudential question of **what quantity and duration of breastfeeding establishes the prohibited degrees of kinship (taḥrīm al-riḍāʿah)**. The narration emphasizes that the milk must provide genuine nourishment — reaching and nourishing the stomach — and must occur **before the age of weaning**, which the scholars have understood in light of the Qur'anic prescription of two years of breastfeeding (al-Baqarah: 233). Imām al-Tirmidhī (raḥimahullāh) affirms the consensus of the majority of scholars that **breastfeeding after the completion of two full years does not establish any marital prohibition**."
1156,Ibn Abbas narrated:“Barfahs husband was a black slave belonging to Banu Al-Mughirah. On the day that Barirah was freed. By Allah! It is as if I can see him in the streets of Al-Madinah behind her. Indeed tears were flowing down his beard while he was trying to get her to chose to stay with him
"1157","“Barfah’s husband was a slave, so the Messenger of Allah let her chose, and she chose herself, and if he was a free man she would not have had a choice.”"
1158,"# Scholarly Translation of the Hadith

**Hannād narrated to us:** Abū Mu'āwiyah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Ibrāhīm, from al-Aswad, from 'Ā'ishah (may Allāh be pleased with her), who said:

*""The husband of Barīrah was a free man, and the Messenger of Allāh (peace and blessings be upon him) gave her the choice.""*

Abū 'Īsā (al-Tirmidhī) said: *""The hadith of 'Ā'ishah is a hasan sahīh (good and authentic) hadith.""*

He then noted: *""Thus narrated Hishām, from his father, from 'Ā'ishah, who said: 'The husband of Barīrah was a slave.'""*

And 'Ikrimah narrated from Ibn 'Abbās (may Allāh be pleased with them both), who said: *""I saw the husband of Barīrah, and he was a slave whose name was Mughīth.""*

And similarly, it was narrated from Ibn 'Umar (may Allāh be pleased with them both).

**The established practice (al-'amal) according to some of the scholars of knowledge is upon this narration.** They said: *""If a female slave is under a free man and she is emancipated, then she has no option (of separation). The option is only granted to her if she is emancipated while she is under a slave.""* This is the position of al-Shāfi'ī, Ahmad, and Isḥāq.

Al-A'mash narrated from Ibrāhīm, from al-Aswad, from 'Ā'ishah (may Allāh be pleased with her), who said: *""The husband of Barīrah was a free man, and the Messenger of Allāh (peace and blessings be upon him) gave her the choice.""*

And Abū 'Awānah narrated this hadith from al-A'mash, from Ibrāhīm, from al-Aswad, from 'Ā'ishah (may Allāh be pleased with her), regarding the story of Barīrah. Al-Aswad said: *""And her husband was a free man.""*

**The established practice (al-'amal) according to some of the scholars of knowledge from the Tābi'īn and those after them is upon this narration.** This is the position of Sufyān al-Thawrī and the people of Kūfah."
"1159","“Barfahs husband was a black slave belonging to Banu Al-Mughirah. On the day that Barirah was freed. By Allah! It is as if I can see him in the streets of Al-Madinah behind her. Indeed tears were flowing down his beard while he was trying to get her to chose to stay with him, but she did not do it.”"
1160,Talq bin Ali narrated that The Messenger of Allah said:“When a man calls his wife to fulfill his need
"1161","“The Prophet saw a woman, then he went to Zainab to fulfill his need and he left. He said: ‘Indeed when the woman enters, she enters in the image of Shaitan. So when one of you sees a woman that he is fascinated with, then let him go to his wife, for indeed with her (his wife) is the same as that which is with her.”"
1162,Abu Hurairah narrated that The Messenger of Allah said:“The most complete of the believers in faith
1163,Sulaiman bin Amr bin Al-Ahwas said:“My father narrated to me that he witnessed the farewell Hajj with the Messenger of Allah. So he thanked and praised Allah and he reminded and gave admonition. He mentioned a story in his narration and he (the Prophet) said: “And indeed I order you to be good to the women
1164,Ali bin Talq narrated that A Bedouin came to the Prophet and said:“O Messenger of Allah! A man among us would be in the desert and a small smell would come from him
1165,Ibn Abbas narrated that The Messenger of Allah said:“Allah will not look at a man who enters a man or a woman in the behind.”
"1166","“My father narrated to me that he witnessed the farewell Hajj with the Messenger of Allah. So he thanked and praised Allah and he reminded and gave admonition. He mentioned a story in his narration and he (the Prophet) said: “And indeed I order you to be good to the women, for they are but captives with you over whom you have no power than that, except if they come with manifest Fahishah (evil behavior). If they do that, then abandon their beds and beat them with a beating that is not harmful. And if they obey you then you have no cause against them. Indeed you have rights over your women, and your women have rights over you. As for your rights over your women, then they must not allow anyone whom you dislike to treat on your bedding (furniture), nor to admit anyone in your home that you dislike. And their rights over you are that you treat them well in clothing them and feeding them.”"
1167,"# Translation of the Hadith

**Narrated by Ahmad ibn Mani' and Hannād**, both of whom said: **Abū Mu'āwiyyah narrated to us**, from **Āṣim al-Aḥwal**, from **Īsā ibn Ḥiṭṭān**, from **Muslim ibn Salām**, from **ʿAlī ibn Ṭalq**, who said:

A Bedouin came to the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, a man among us may be in the open desert, and he may pass wind, and water may be scarce."" The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said:

**""When any one of you passes wind, let him perform wudu. And do not approach women from their posteriors, for Allah is not shy of the truth.""**

He (the narrator) said: **In this chapter (there are narrations) from ʿUmar, Khuzaymah ibn Thābit, Ibn ʿAbbās, and Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with them all.**

**Abū ʿĪsā (al-Tirmidhī) said:** The hadith of ʿAlī ibn Ṭalq is a *ḥasan* (good) hadith.

**And I heard Muḥammad (al-Bukhārī) say:** ""I do not know of any other hadith from ʿAlī ibn Ṭalq from the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, besides this single hadith, and I do not know this hadith from the narration of Ṭalq ibn ʿAlī al-Suḥaymī. And it is as though he (al-Bukhārī) considered that this was another man from among the Companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him.""

---

*This hadith is recorded in Sunan al-Tirmidhī and addresses two matters of Islamic jurisprudence: the obligation of renewing wudu upon passing wind, and the prohibition of approaching one's wife from her posterior, with the Prophet ﷺ affirming that Allah, the Most High, is not shy of declaring what is right and true.*"
1168,Abu Hurairah narrated that The Messenger of Allah said:“Allah becomes jealous and the believer becomes jealous. Allah’s jealousy occurs when a believer does what He has made unlawful for him.”
1169,Abu Sa’eed Al-Khudri narrated that The Messenger of Allah said:“It is not lawful for a woman who believers in Allah and the Last Day to travel on a trip that is three days or more
1170,Abu Hurairah narrated that The Messenger of Allah said:“A woman is not to travel the distance of a day and a night unless she is accompanied by someone who is a Mahram.”
1171,Uqbah bin Amir narrated that The Messenger of Allah said:“Beware of entering upon women.” So a man from the Ansar said: ‘”O Messenger of Allah! What do you think about Hamu? So he said: “The Hamu is death.”
"1172","“It is not lawful for a woman who believers in Allah and the Last Day to travel on a trip that is three days or more, unless she is accompanied by her father, her brother, her husband, her son, or someone who is a Mahram to her.”"
1173,"Narrated by Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""A woman shall not travel for a distance of a day and a night unless she is accompanied by a mahram (a male relative whom marriage is permanently prohibited)."" Abu Isa (al-Tirmidhi) said: This is a hasan sahih hadith."
1174,Mu’adh bin Jabal narrated that The Prophet said:“No woman annoys her husband in the world except that his wife among the Al-Huril-Ain said: ‘Do not annoy him
